.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH tapectl-collection-run 1  "tapectl-collection-run " 
.SH NAME
tapectl\-collection\-run \- Execute one batch: stage every unit in it once, write one session to the destination label, then release staging IF that copy already satisfies every unit\*(Aqs resolved `min_copies` — otherwise staging is retained for the further copies still needed. Targets a single collection (unlike `sync`/`status`/`plan`, which sweep every configured collection) since a batch write is a real, one\-shot tape action
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtapectl collection run\fR <\fB\-\-collection\fR> [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-batch\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-label\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-\-device\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-\-prewrite\-hash\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-home\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Execute one batch: stage every unit in it once, write one session to the destination label, then release staging IF that copy already satisfies every unit\*(Aqs resolved `min_copies` — otherwise staging is retained for the further copies still needed. Targets a single collection (unlike `sync`/`status`/`plan`, which sweep every configured collection) since a batch write is a real, one\-shot tape action
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-collection\fR \fI<COLLECTION>\fR
Collection name
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output in JSON format
.TP
\fB\-\-batch\fR \fI<BATCH>\fR [default: 0]
Which batch to execute (0 = first). Numbered against THIS run\*(Aqs own budget — the destination `\-\-label` volumes\*(Aq recorded capacity, not the drive\*(Aqs generation. This matches `collection plan`\*(Aqs ordering only when `plan` was run (`\-\-generation <GEN>`) for the generation those volumes were actually initialised as; otherwise the batch reviewed in `plan` is not necessarily batch N here
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be done without making changes. Commands that cannot preview refuse the flag rather than ignore it
.TP
\fB\-\-label\fR \fI<LABELS>\fR
Destination volume label — already `volume init`\*(Aqd on its own cartridge. Exactly one: tapectl drives no changer, so it cannot write a second copy without a human swapping cartridges, and a batch run has no point where that swap could happen. More than one is refused. For a second and further copy, swap in the next cartridge after this run finishes and use `tapectl volume write <label>` directly against the same staged data
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Enable verbose output
.TP
\fB\-\-device\fR \fI<DEVICE>\fR
Tape device (by\-id path). Defaults to the only configured drive; required when more than one is configured
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
No live progress on stderr. Long operations (stage create, volume write/resume/verify/read\-slices, restore) still write their session log under <home>/logs/
.TP
\fB\-\-prewrite\-hash\fR
See `volume write \-\-prewrite\-hash`: full\-hash every staged slice before the tape moves, for every copy this run writes
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Skip ADR\-0008 Tier\-2 confirmation prompts. It never reaches a Tier\-3 refusal — those are facts, not risks to accept
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
Path to config file.

NOTE: on its own this ALSO relocates the whole tapectl home to the config file\*(Aqs parent directory — database, keys, staging, stage reports. That is how every test harness gets an isolated home, so it still works, but it is surprising enough that it now warns. Use \-\-home when you mean "operate on a different archive", and \-\-config only to point at a config file inside that home.
.TP
\fB\-\-home\fR \fI<HOME>\fR
tapectl home directory: database, keys, catalogs, stage\-reports, logs.

Defaults to ~/.tapectl. The config file is taken from <home>/config.toml unless \-\-config overrides it. Also settable as TAPECTL_HOME.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
